> 1) I have a wand of polymorph, how should I use it? I heard about polypiling
> and read the spoiler but I'm still confused on how many I should put before
> a golem is generated. Also I only have items from all the dwarf or gnome
> corpses. Will that hurt my chance of getting things like Dragon Scale Mail
> from polypiling (assuming it's even possible)?
	I'm not certain about the optimum amount of items to polypile to 
prevent from merging and golem generation, but IIRC, some stacks of 4 on 
each square(a wand can hit some five to six squares, I believe) is good 
as a thumb rule. Non-magic objects will normally not polymorph into 
magic objects; so you should try to get "useless" magic objects - like 
for example rings of hunger or amulets of change - for polypiling. You 
should also be aware that polymorphed items retain their enchantment - 
knowing this, you can do interesting things with elven armor :).
	Dragon scale mails are AFAIK not randomly generated and thus can't be 
made with polymorph. You can, however, get nice and useful
cloaks, boots, helmets, etc.

> 2) How do I kill Blue Jellies? My pet will hit them for a while, suffer the
> "The large dog suddenly feels cold!" and the Blue Jelly will INCREASE in hp.
> I once had to quit because they blocked off the down stairs and I couldn't
> proceed.
	Obviously, cold resistance will help here. If you have it, you will 
only feel "a mild chill" and you can hit it to your heart's content. 
You'll know that you have cold resistance when you feel "full of hot 
air" (generally, eat things associated with cold, frost and winter(which 
includes blue jellies) - consult the spoilers for more specific 
information). There's also a ring of cold resistance, but it's really 
not too hard to get the intrinsic.
	If you haven't got the resistance, attack it from a distance. In a 
pinch, you can use rocks as they are cheap and easily available, but if 
you're skilled with for example daggers or attack spells, use that.

> 3) How do pet levelling work? My large dog is level 9 and stops gaining any
> gains from killing anymore. I heard it was each monster kill gives your pet
> 1 hp but then what is the level for?
	Certain monsters(or perhaps all monsters?) have caps on experience 
level; 9 seems to be the limit for "natural" level gain for the starting 
pet types. I don't know if the pet level affects attack strength, but it 
does affect HP - each level gain gives a HP boost, even though they do 
gain HP for each monster kill as well.
	Somebody might be able to give a more specific answer :).

> 4) Is exercising stats important? My constitution rises up on it's own often
> but my dex keeps dropping because I'm satiated from eating boulders, how can
> I stop this?
	For one thing; you could go to the root of the problem - you shouldn't 
be satiated. With boulders and stone to flesh, you could do a neat 
little trick with one charge from a wand of polymorph, instead of simply 
eating the (presumably stone-to-fleshed) boulders: Break a boulder, or 
several boulders, until you have a decent number of rocks. 
Stone-to-flesh the rocks and you have meatballs. These are very small 
and not much good as food; but if you polymorph them, you might get a 
large amount of nice food such as slime molds or lembas wafers. If you 
carry these around, you can eat when you're hungry instead of going 
around being satiated.
	That said, stat loss can be cured by applying a unicorn horn, and 
you'll want to max out most stats - this really isn't as hard as it 
sounds, considering the blessed effects of certain potions.

> 5) How do I get more USEFUL items, such as wands, potions of scrolls? Nymphs
> drop object detection, which is sort of worthless and paper golems drop
> scrolls but they seem to be pretty rare and the scrolls are unlablled. Wands
> seem to be generally rare, is there a chance of a monster dropping a wand of
> wishing?
	All of the mentioned item types (except the wand of wishing) can be 
acquired by polypiling, which you've already mentioned, so I won't 
mention that here.
	For potions, there is alchemy; dipping certain potions in certain other 
potions produces useful results. Look up the potion spoiler for the 
details. Note that when it says that (A+B)=>C, you could
have 20 of potion A and one of potion B; dipping your 20 potions in your 
one potion would produce 20 of potion C. This is naturally very useful. 
Alchemy can be used to easily manipulate your amount of HP/PW, as soon 
as you find the right potions.
	Scrolls(and spellbooks) can be written with a magic marker - this is 
what blank scrolls are useful for. You can write known scrolls with 
(AFAIK) 100% chance of success, and unknown scrolls with a chance of 
failure depending on your luck and whether or not you are a wizard(if 
you aren't a wizard, trying to write unknown scrolls is likely to be 
costly and unsuccessful).

> 6) Finally, how do I counter against level teleport and trapdoors traps?
> They tend to seperate me from my pet, and I depend on him to live, since my
> offensive power as a healer isn't that great.
	There are "technical counters" like levitation and magic resistance or 
teleport control, but unless you're playing pacifist or semi-pacifist, 
it does seem as if you should learn some no-pet survival skills. Be 
non-burdened and fast, so that you can evade monsters, and try to get 
upstairs as fast as possible(assuming you've been dropped deeper into 
the dungeon, which is generally the deadliest). As you are a healer, you 
should be able to cast healing spells on yourself in a pinch. Other 
useful items when in this situation are scrolls of magic mapping (to 
find the stairs quickly) and any objects that will make you
teleport or change level - are you aware, for example, of the special 
effects of cursed potions of gain level?

